some breaking news to bring you and the  bbc has today published a review that  was set up to establish the facts  around the decision to appoint martin  bashir as religious affairs  correspondent in september 2016 a role  that  was promoted to religion editor in 2018.  now this  review looked at three questions was the  recruitment process intended to find the  right person for the role  as opposed to being predetermined what  did those who appointed martin beshear  know about his previous misconduct in  relation to the 1995 panorama interview  and his other public controversies and  in what circumstances was  martin beshear's role regraded in 2018.  let's bring in our royal correspondent  rhianna mills who's been having a look  at that  review and rhiannon what has it  concluded  so i think it's worth reminding everyone  that this has actually come  as a result of that dyson report that  was released  at the end of may which was looking at  exactly how martin beshear had managed  to  uh secure that really explosive  interview with princess diana that  panorama interview back  uh in 1995 and through that dyson report  they found that martin beshear had  in essence lied he'd used fake bank  statements  in order to convince earl spencer so  princess diana's brother  to introduce him to diana and made them  both feel that they were being spied on  in some sense so in essence he used that  to secure the interview  it was then within the bbc found out  that he had used fake documents  and that was then covered up now as a  result of that  report there were also then questions  about why exactly  after martin beshear had left the bbc  wyatt was decided to re-hire him in 2016  under the role of religious affairs  correspondent  now this latest review in essence has  found that when it came to the interview  process  the bbc did all the right things the  person behind the report says  in my view the recruitment process for  the religious affairs correspondent  was targeted at finding the right person  for the role  although there were some shortcomings in  the process by which he was re-employed  i am satisfied that he was ultimately  appointed because his knowledge  and experience were considered to be the  best match to the requirements for the  role  at the time he goes on to say i have  found no evidence that martin beshear  was rehired  to contain or cover up the events  surrounding the 1995 panorama program  in my view that theory is entirely  unfounded  it also goes on to say that those who  were involved in the recruitment process  actually were not aware of the fact that  martin beshear had used these fake  documents and lied  uh surrounding the panorama interview  there's also  uh in the press release we've had from  the bbc a statement from the bbc  director general tim davey in which he  thanks ken macquarie for carrying out  this report  and says it finds the recruitment  process was targeted to find the right  person for the role  and it was conducted in good faith but  it does go on to say that while the  report find processes were largely  followed at the time it is clear we need  to reflect on the findings to ensure  consistent  best practice is applied in our  recruitment and also i think this is a  really interesting line it says finally  it is without doubt that had the  organization been aware  of what is now publicly known because of  the dyson report  martin beshear would never have been  reappointed  so in many ways this is a good report  for the bbc  saying that actually when it came to  re-employing martin beshear they went  through all of the right processes but  once again  reaffirming that if those involved in  the recruitment process had known that  he previously lied  and acted in the way that he had the  deceitful way that he had to secure the  panorama interview  he simply would not have been  re-employed in that role
 

